So I cross-checked stable and what you say is pretty blatantly false, and it doesn't take access to source to prove it.

Here's a beatmap: [a - a.zip](https://github.com/ppy/osu/files/14851155/a.-.a.zip) (rename to `.osz` to import).

Inside the storyboard, I put this:

```
Sprite,Foreground,Centre,"bug.png",320,240
 F,0,1200,4000,0,1
 F,0,2500,3000,1,0
```

Stable does this:

https://github.com/ppy/osu/assets/20418176/ee087b3f-3bd7-4672-a790-236935c9d415

and lazer does this:

https://github.com/ppy/osu/assets/20418176/e1b05a27-ca7d-4db9-b87a-87e9cb1427db

The long and short of it is, no, stable does not discard the first transform if it sees there are two concurrent fade transforms. It discards _the second one_. As in, the _first transform is played out start to end_ and the second one is _ignored_.

As far as I can tell, the stable code for this looks a bit like so:

```csharp
            // First check for active transformations
            foreach (Transformation t in Transformations)
            {
                if (t.Time1 >= time || t.Time2 > time)
                {
                    hasFuture = true;

                    if (t.Loop && t.CurrentLoopCount > 0 && !SpriteManager.Current.ForwardPlayOptimisations)
                        hasUpdateableLoops = true;
                    if (t.Time1 > time)
                        continue;
                }

                if (t.Time2 <= time)
                {
                    hasPast = true;

                    if (t.Loop && (t.MaxLoopCount == 0 || t.CurrentLoopCount < t.MaxLoopCount - 1))
                        hasUpdateableLoops = true;
                    if (t.Time2 < time)
                        continue;
                }

                shouldDraw = true;

                switch (t.Type)
                {
                    case TransformationType.Fade:
                        if (hasFade) break;
                        Alpha = OsuMathHelper.TweenValues(t.StartFloat, t.EndFloat, time, t.Time1, t.Time2, t.Easing);
                        hasFade = true;
                        break;
```

(https://github.com/peppy/osu-stable-reference/blob/e53980dd76857ee899f66ce519ba1597e7874f28/osu!/Graphics/Sprites/pSprite.cs#L532-L563)

Long and short of it is, if there are multiple concurrent transforms from a single group, the _first one_ wins. Now the fact that the display is different is _two_ bugs, one of which is in the _application_ of the fades and thus out of scope here, but it still makes this variant of the optimisation _wrong_, since the lifetime end of the sprite will be calculated to be 3000 with this code, while in fact it should be no less than 4000.

I'm somewhat miffed that I am the one having to verify this experimentally. Yes I may have stable access but all it takes to check this is having access to stable and making a test case to test this.
